title: Ship directions — nautical directions block (ADR-267 acceptance 8)
story: nautical
description: The owner condition on ADR-267 D12 — a directions block is
  per-action vocabulary, never compass-hardcoded. `sail port` (expanded
  slotted form), `sail sb` (alias), and bare `starboard` (standalone form) all
  reach the sailing action's body with the RIGHT canonical direction (word
  binding renders in the phrase, ADR-275 D2). `sail aft` proves word equality
  in a refuse-when arm (ADR-275 D4); bare `sail` proves the refuse-without arm
  over a semantic binding (ADR-275 D1).

---

# Expanded slotted form: the canonical word reaches the body
> sail port
[OK: contains "the sloop swings port"]

# Alias form: the CANONICAL (starboard), not the alias (sb), is the binding
> sail sb
[OK: contains "the sloop swings starboard"]

# Standalone bare-direction form from the bare `the direction` pattern
> starboard
[OK: contains "the sloop swings starboard"]

# Standalone alias
> p
[OK: contains "the sloop swings port"]

# Word equality in a refuse-when arm: aft is refused by name
> sail aft
[OK: contains "never aft"]

# Bare verb: the refuse-without arm fires over the unbound semantic binding
> sail
[OK: contains "Sail which way?"]

# Fore still sails (only aft is refused)
> sail fore
[OK: contains "the sloop swings fore"]
